00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.485
SPEAKER 2: Can you
recite the Honor Code?

00:00:10.485 --> 00:00:12.110
ANDREW: A Hillsdale
College Student

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.070
is honorable in deed-- frick.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:14.850
OK.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.425
REAGAN: --is honorable
in word and deed.

00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:19.800
LUKE: A Hillsdale
College student

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.270
is honorable in word and deed.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.220
Lie?

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.018
ANNIE: Michelle will
probably know it.

00:00:27.018 --> 00:00:28.560
MICHELLE: A Hillsdale
College student

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.310
is honorable in conduct,
honest in word and deed,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.765
dutiful in study and service.

00:00:33.765 --> 00:00:37.980
LUKE: And he attends classes.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.500
REAGAN: Freedom,
because liberal arts.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.555
MICHELLE: And respectful
of the rights of others.

00:00:42.555 --> 00:00:44.880
REAGAN: Brushing
your teeth, it's

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.390
good to brush your
teeth, people.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.040
LUKE: He's not late to meetings.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.670
REAGAN: OK, I need
my next line, please.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.300
MICHELLE: Rising to education?

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:52.800
No.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.630
SPEAKER 2: Can you
recite the Honor Code?

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.303
BLAKE: Yes, I can.

00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:58.470
A Hillsdale College student
is honorable in conduct,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.690
honest in word and deed,
dutiful in study and service,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.340
and respectful of
the rights of others.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.739
Through education, a student
rises to self-government.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:05.665
SPEAKER 2: Nice.

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:06.337
BLAKE: Thanks.

00:01:06.337 --> 00:01:07.879
SPEAKER 1: Yeah,
that was [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:08.785
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:08.785 --> 00:01:11.113
BLAKE: I have to be able
to do it for my job.

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:12.780
That's how we start
all of our meetings.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.822
We recite the Honor Code.

00:01:13.822 --> 00:01:16.620
REAGAN: I was, you know,
a senior class secretary.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.235
I need to have these
things memorized.

00:01:21.235 --> 00:01:23.820
ANNIE: It's incredible
how these things-- like,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.550
how the words of the
Honor Code, how I don't

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.470
remember the specific phrasing.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.283
But, like, it's
integrated into my life.

00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:34.700
LUKE: When I first heard that
Hillsdale had an Honor Code,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.492
it was that Dr. Arnn's
speech that he gave

00:01:36.492 --> 00:01:37.700
at the beginning of the year.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.560
And it was a pretty,
like, strange, sort

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.240
of interesting
thing that I'm not

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.488
really used to having,
like, codes given to me.

00:01:44.488 --> 00:01:46.280
I went to a public high
school, so we never

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.270
had anything like that.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.420
KAMRYN: I think I was on, like,
a little tour around campus.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.290
And we were down
by the cafeteria,

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.360
and they said something
about, oh, yeah.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.670
People just leave their
backpacks, and their Mac books,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.920
and their wallets,
and their phones

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.420
everywhere, because we have an
Honor Code, so no one touches

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.270
anything.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.710
I had never heard anything
like an Honor Code

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.750
ever, especially around
a college campus.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.625
ANDREW: The first time
I heard that Hillsdale

00:02:10.625 --> 00:02:13.963
had an Honor Code, my
older brother came here.

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:15.380
When I heard that,
I was like, OK,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.820
he's going to a weird school.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.422
But that's what I expected.

00:02:18.422 --> 00:02:19.130
He's a weird guy.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.680
REAGAN: The first time I was
on campus hearing Dr. Arnn

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.980
talk about the Honor Code,
and the good, the true,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.090
and the beautiful, and I cried.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.450
I cried during that lecture.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.510
It was a little pathetic.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.950
But it wasn't, like,
as dramatic as I am.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.160
It was just like a
little, a little tear.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.560
ANNIE: The Honor Code
just, like, permeates out

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.420
into, like,
everything that we do.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.160
Like, you can see
it in the classroom,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.620
in the way that we
respect our professors,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.170
in the way that we respect
other students, in the way

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.710
that we do our homework, in
the way that, like, we want

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.440
to help one another,
and we don't cheat,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.238
and we're not cutthroat.

00:02:54.238 --> 00:02:56.780
We're not trying to, like, tear
other students down or, like,

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.010
manipulate them.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.470
But we, like, we want
to help one another,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.490
and do it, like, in a way
that's honest and truthful.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:07.700
MICHELLE: Yeah, I think when
you view it as a list of rules,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.510
you miss out on
all the good parts.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.690
When it's a way of
life, you choose things

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.120
because you love
them, and because you

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.120
see them as actually meaningful
and beneficial to you,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.800
not because you have to.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.635
It gains meaning, and
it becomes personal.

00:03:21.635 --> 00:03:23.930
VINS: Coming to college
and being independent,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.770
you are away from your parents.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.300
You have a blank sheet to
develop friends and develop

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.680
a reputation.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.220
So taking ownership of
that was very important.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.490
It took me a while to
develop that ownership.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:40.220
MICHELLE: Another phrase you
hear all the time at Hillsdale

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.290
is that college is partnership.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.520
When you are aiming
at such a high ideal

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.660
and wanting to be people
of excellence and virtue,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.670
you can't do that alone.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.610
So having a community
of people that hold you

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.630
to a standard, that
encourage you, that love you,

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.648
that teach you, that's powerful.

00:03:59.648 --> 00:04:01.190
LUKE: So I think
the best way to take

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.860
ownership of who you're going
to be in your four years here

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.110
is to find a community
of your peers

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.460
that can hold you accountable.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.585
And that when you have to
make those tough choices,

00:04:09.585 --> 00:04:11.960
not only you're holding yourself
accountable to something

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.460
that the college
expects you to do,

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.668
but you're also holding
yourself accountable to a set

00:04:15.668 --> 00:04:17.839
of standards that another
community at this campus

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.637
has put forward for
you to live up to.

00:04:19.637 --> 00:04:21.470
BLAKE: Some of the
relationships that you're

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.840
going to build in the first
three months at college,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.210
you're going to be like, how
did I not know these people

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.020
my entire life?

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.350
And with that, you have to
recognize the strength that

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.240
is found in those
relationships, and you

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.850
have to be willing to call
people out on certain things,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.345
and understand that you want
to be the type of friend

00:04:38.345 --> 00:04:40.595
to other people that you
would like them to be to you.

00:04:40.595 --> 00:04:42.650
MICHELLE: Friendship
really comes to a head

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.920
when there's conflict or you
know that your friend would

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.870
be hurt if you spoke truth.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:52.130
I think in those moments,
it's selfish as a friend

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.110
to just tell them
what's comfortable,

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.525
to preserve your friendship now
and hurt them in the long run.

00:04:57.525 --> 00:04:59.420
LUKE: And it shows
real, true bravery

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.400
to stand up and help somebody
in their time of need.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.180
And it kind of shows the exact
opposite of that cowardice,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.910
if you're kind of shrinking
away from something

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.000
that looks wrong, or, OK,
maybe I should say something.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.200
But, they probably know
what they're doing.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.000
But no, if you see
something wrong,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.570
step up, and say something,
and stand up for yourself,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.112
and stand up for
others that need it.

00:05:19.112 --> 00:05:23.430
ANDREW: There's certainly a lot
of shenaniganery that goes on.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:28.400
But we do hold each
other to be honorable.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.982
It is so integral to the very
fabric of what Hillsdale is.

00:05:32.982 --> 00:05:34.940
REAGAN: We have a lot of
responsibilities here.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.090
And sometimes, we subject
ourselves to this,

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.390
we're still children because
we're still in college idea,

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.948
or it's our only chance to act
like kids, which means reckless

00:05:44.948 --> 00:05:46.490
and making mistakes
that you're going

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.782
to act like you don't regret
for the rest of your life.

00:05:48.782 --> 00:05:53.060
And honor matters because
when you are honorable,

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.170
you are unified.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.590
You don't have a dissonance
between what you want to be

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:58.370
and what you are.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.060
And it's the little pieces
every day that you choose.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:05.930
It's decisions, decisions,
decisions, and they shape you,

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:09.020
and they form you, and that
is what makes you honorable,

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.885
and all of the things that are
outlined in our Honor Code.

00:06:11.885 --> 00:06:14.570
LUKE: I think the person I
want to be when I leave here

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.510
is somebody that I looked
up to when I was a freshman.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.620
They exhibited traits of a man
to me when I was a young man.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.040
They showed what it meant
to approach work dutifully.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.530
They showed what it meant
to stick by your word.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.100
And seeing those
things exemplified

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.410
in somebody older than me, I
wanted to emulate those people

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:33.443
when I was younger.

00:06:33.443 --> 00:06:35.860
And I think that's just an
amazing thing that we get here.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.220
ANNIE: I think it's all about
trust, the faculty and staff

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.710
trusting the students,
and the students

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.380
trusting the faculty and staff.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:48.010
And when those two things
come together, then, like,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.948
you can't see it until
you just, like, trust,

00:06:49.948 --> 00:06:52.240
and you have faith, and then
something really beautiful

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:52.950
comes of that.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:56.440
ANDREW: Character is
important because it is life,

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.890
because it's how you
interact with the world,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.490
because it's who you are.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.600
So making sure you
have good character

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.290
is something that's incredibly
important, because it is--

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.410
I mean, yeah it is you.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.570
MICHELLE: A Hillsdale
College student

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:19.290
is honorable in conduct,
honest in word and deed,

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.140
dutiful in study and
service, and respectful

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.770
of the rights of others,
rising to education--

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:26.730
no?

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.148
Yes, through education
rising to self-government.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:30.144
SPEAKER 2: Yeah.

00:07:30.144 --> 00:07:31.638
MICHELLE: Did I get it?

00:07:31.638 --> 00:07:32.640
SPEAKER 2: Boom.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:33.265
MICHELLE: Boom.

00:07:33.265 --> 00:07:34.980
Oh, man I memorized
it last night.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.330
[LAUGHTER]

